    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Telephone numbers are now checked against their country code: a `+61` number must have exactly 9 digits after the country code and a `+1` number exactly 10. A wrong length is rejected with 400. The full test suite passes (5 tests, 0 failures), including the new acceptance test that posts `+61 123` and expects a 400.\n\n**Changes:**\n- **`OwnerTelephone.java`:** After the existing E.164 format check, a new step looks up the number's country code in a small table (`61` \u2192 9, `1` \u2192 10) and throws `InvalidTelephoneException` if the length is wrong. Numbers without a leading `+` get `+61` added first, so they go through the same check.\n- **`InvalidTelephoneException.java`:** Added a constructor that takes a custom message. The error now says which country code failed and how many digits it needs, e.g. \"\u2026country code '+61' must have exactly 9 national digits\". It goes through the existing 400 handler, so the response format is unchanged.\n- **`openapi.yml`:** Added the per-country rule to the telephone field's description.\n\nCountry codes not in the table still only get the general check of 8\u201315 digits after the `+`. To cover another country, add one entry to the table. The existing `+1` seed owners (e.g. `+16085551023`) have 10 digits, so they still pass.",
